    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:569e72e117968fcc",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "a0cb45b49c02ec858d4c4deb9ac18880abf41f4edf39e327943662d5a65d8911",
      "text": "Day 2 · After breakfast, a full-day city tour of Quito and the Equator Line (about 8 hours). Founded in the 16th century on the ruins of an Inca city, at about 2,850 m, Quito keeps one of the best-preserved historic centres in Latin America, a showcase of the Quito School of Art, and was among the very first places inscribed on UNESCO’s World Heritage List. On foot through the Old Town: San Francisco church and square, the Plaza Grande with the Cathedral, the Presidential Palace and City Hall, and the Jesuit church of La Compañía, with its carved façade and golden interior; then the view from El Panecillo, crowned by the Winged Virgin of Quito. In the afternoon, the Middle of the World: the Ciudad Mitad del Mundo, whose 30-metre monument commemorates the French Geodesic Mission of 1736 (GPS later placed the true equator about 240 m away), and the Intiñán Museum, with experiments on the equator and displays on ancient Ecuadorian cultures. Overnight at Hotel Finlandia 4★. (B)",
      "provenance": {
